1
00:03:13,846 --> 00:03:19,846
In the future, intelligence is extinct.

2
00:06:27,692 --> 00:06:33,692
Idiocracy is a comedy, science fiction, adventure film directed by Mike Judge.

3
00:09:41,538 --> 00:09:47,538
To test its top-secret Human Hibernation Project, the Pentagon picks the most average Americans it can find - an
Army private and a prostitute - and sends them to the year 2505 after a series of freak events.

4
00:12:55,384 --> 00:13:01,384
But when they arrive, they find a civilization so
dumbed-down that they're the smartest people around.

5
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:15,230
Joe Bauers, played by Luke Wilson.

6
00:19:23,076 --> 00:19:29,076
Rita, played by Maya Rudolph.

7
00:22:36,923 --> 00:22:42,923
Frito, played by Dax Shepard.

8
00:25:50,769 --> 00:25:56,769
President Camacho, played by Terry Crews.

9
00:29:04,615 --> 00:29:10,615
Secretary of Defense, played by Anthony 'Citric' Campos.

10
00:32:18,461 --> 00:32:24,461
Secretary of State, played by David Herman.

11
00:35:32,307 --> 00:35:38,307
Idiocracy is a 2006 American science fiction comedy film co-produced and directed by Mike Judge
from a screenplay written by Judge and Etan Cohen based on a story written by Judge.

12
00:38:46,153 --> 00:38:52,153
The plot follows United States Army librarian Joe Bauers and prostitute
Rita, who undergo a government hibernation experiment.

13
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Joe and Rita awake five hundred years later in a
dystopian anti-intellectual society.

14
00:45:13,846 --> 00:45:19,846
The cast includes Luke Wilson, Maya Rudolph, Dax Shepard, Terry Crews,
David Herman, Justin Long, Andrew Wilson, and Brad Jordan.

15
00:48:27,692 --> 00:48:33,692
The concept of Idiocracy dates back to a concept Judge envisioned in 1996.

16
00:51:41,538 --> 00:51:47,538
Judge finished a script with the working title 3001 in
2001, rewriting the film a year later.

17
00:54:55,384 --> 00:55:01,384
Filming took place throughout 2004 at Austin Studios and other cities in Texas.

18
00:58:09,230 --> 00:58:15,230
Idiocracy serves as a social satire that touches on issues including anti-intellectualism,
commercialism, consumerism, dysgenics, voluntary childlessness, and overpopulation.

19
01:01:23,076 --> 01:01:29,076
20th Century Fox was hesitant to promote the film, refusing to grant it a
wide release, and did not screen the film for critics.

20
01:04:36,923 --> 01:04:42,923
The decision not to market Idiocracy was seen as unexpected, following the
success of Office Space (1999), and led to speculation.

21
01:07:50,769 --> 01:07:56,769
According to Crews, the film's satirical depiction of corporations made the film financially unviable, while Judge attributed 20th Century Fox's decision to negative
test screenings; Judge stated that 20th Century Fox believed that the film would develop a cult following through its DVD release, similar to Office Space.

22
01:11:04,615 --> 01:11:10,615
The film was released in the United States on September 1, 2006.

23
01:14:18,461 --> 01:14:24,461
Despite its lack of a major theatrical release, which resulted in a $495,000 gross at the box
office, the film received positive reviews from critics and has since become a cult film.

24
01:17:32,307 --> 01:17:38,307
The film explores themes of future, army, prostitute, usa president, congress.

25
01:20:46,153 --> 01:20:52,153
The tone is lighthearted and dark and thought_provoking.
